1
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Where everything seems possible and nothing is what it seems.

2
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Labyrinth is a adventure, family, fantasy film directed by Jim Henson.

3
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:12,000
Frustrated with babysitting on yet another weekend night, Sarah, a teenager with an
active imagination, summons the Goblins to take her baby stepbrother away.

4
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:34,000
When little Toby actually disappears, Sarah must follow him into a
fantastical world to rescue him from the Goblin King.

5
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:56,000
Guarding his castle is the labyrinth itself, a twisted maze of deception,
populated with outrageous characters and unknown dangers.

6
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:18,000
Jareth, played by David Bowie.

7
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:40,000
Sarah, played by Jennifer Connelly.

8
00:26:56,000 --> 00:27:02,000
Toby, played by Toby Froud.

9
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:24,000
Stepmother, played by Shelley Thompson.

10
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:46,000
Father, played by Christopher Malcolm.

11
00:37:02,000 --> 00:37:08,000
Hoggle / Goblin (voice), played by Brian Henson.

12
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:30,000
Labyrinth is a 1986 musical fantasy film directed by Jim Henson from a screenplay
by Terry Jones based on a story conceived by Henson and Dennis Lee.

13
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:52,000
A co-production between Henson Associates and Lucasfilm with George Lucas serving as executive producer, the film stars Jennifer Connelly as
teenager Sarah and David Bowie as Jareth, and follows Sarah's journeys through a maze to save her baby brother from the Goblin King.

14
00:47:08,000 --> 00:47:14,000
Labyrinth started as a collaboration between Henson and Brian Froud
following their previous collaboration The Dark Crystal (1982).

15
00:50:30,000 --> 00:50:36,000
Jones of Monty Python wrote the first draft of the film's script early
in 1984, drawing on Froud's sketches for inspiration.

16
00:53:52,000 --> 00:53:58,000
The screenplay underwent several revisions by Laura Phillips, Lucas, Lee, and Elaine
May—although Jones received the film's sole screenwriting credit.

17
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:20,000
It was shot from April to September 1985 on location in Upper Nyack, Piermont, and Haverstraw,
New York, and at Elstree Studios and West Wycombe Park in the United Kingdom.

18
01:00:36,000 --> 01:00:42,000
The film's fantastical creatures were designed by Froud
and created by Jim Henson's Creature Shop.

19
01:03:58,000 --> 01:04:04,000
The New York Times reported that Labyrinth had a budget of $25 million.

20
01:07:20,000 --> 01:07:26,000
The film underperformed at the United States box office,
grossing $12.9 million during its US theatrical run.

21
01:10:42,000 --> 01:10:48,000
However, it was a success in the United Kingdom and
overseas, grossing over $34 million worldwide.

22
01:14:04,000 --> 01:14:10,000
Labyrinth was first met with a mixed critical response upon its release, which contributed
to a difficult period of Henson's career, according to his son Brian Henson.

23
01:17:26,000 --> 01:17:32,000
It was the last feature film that Henson directed, and over the
years it has been re-evaluated by many critics.

24
01:20:48,000 --> 01:20:54,000
A success on home video and television broadcasts,
Labyrinth has gained a large cult following.

25
01:24:10,000 --> 01:24:16,000
The film has been adapted into a variety of media, including
books, video games, board games and comics.

26
01:27:32,000 --> 01:27:38,000
Tokyopop published a four-volume comic sequel Return to Labyrinth between 2006 and 2010, and Archaia
Entertainment published a comic prequel Labyrinth: Coronation between 2018 and 2019.

27
01:30:54,000 --> 01:31:00,000
In January 2016, it was announced that a sequel was in development.

28
01:34:16,000 --> 01:34:22,000
The film explores themes of rescue, race against time, maze, castle, musical.

29
01:37:38,000 --> 01:37:44,000
The tone is mind_bending.
